# Tables the sweep knows are written and never read, each with the reason it is excused.
#
# A table listed here does not fail the sweep. A table NOT listed here, that the sweep finds
# written and never read, does — that is the ratchet. Removing a line is how a table stops
# being excused, and the sweep will then insist somebody either reads it or drops it.
#
# Format: <table> <reference> <reason>

# Approved as Next 1 on #139: read the craft and barter data to answer "worth making" per
# built station and "cheapest route" on every hideout and quest requirement row. The payload
# already carries station, level, duration, tool inputs, trader and minTraderLevel.
crafts               #139-Next-1   reader approved, not yet built
craft_requirements   #139-Next-1   reader approved, not yet built
craft_outputs        #139-Next-1   reader approved, not yet built
barters              #139-Next-1   reader approved, not yet built
barter_requirements  #139-Next-1   reader approved, not yet built
barter_outputs       #139-Next-1   reader approved, not yet built
trader_levels        #139-Next-1   reader approved, not yet built; also #146 for trader names
trader_offers        #139-Next-1   reader approved, not yet built

# Surfaced by fixing this sweep's own DELETE blind spot. #174 proposed dropping the first two.
# They cannot be dropped, and the reason is the same for both: each is the parent row of a
# table that IS read, and foreign keys are on.
#
#   task_objectives.task_id     REFERENCES tasks(id)
#   item_category_membership.category_id REFERENCES item_categories(id)
#
# SqliteRequirementCatalog joins task_objectives, and SqliteItemRepository reads
# item_category_membership. Dropping either parent means rebuilding a child table to remove
# the constraint, and stopping the writes means the child inserts fail outright. So "written
# and never read" is true of the columns and false of the rows: the rows are what makes the
# read data insertable.
#
# This is the sweep reporting exactly what it was built to report and the answer being "keep".
tasks                #174          parent of task_objectives, which is read; FK, cannot drop
item_categories      #174          parent of item_category_membership, which is read; FK, cannot drop
# task_objectives and task_objective_items are NOT here: the sweep says they are read, and
# listing them failed the stale check, which is the ratchet working in the other direction.
#
# This one is genuinely unreferenced, and the answer is to read it rather than drop it: an
# import that silently discards entries is worth a line somebody can see.
quest_catalog_orphans #174         the import records what it refused and nothing shows it
